          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is generalized anxiety disorder, and how is it different from ordinary worry?",
          "answer": "**What it is:** Generalized anxiety disorder is persistent, excessive worry about a range of everyday matters. It happens on more days than not for at least six months, and the person finds it hard to control. The worry moves from topic to topic rather than attaching to one situation. It often comes with restlessness, tiring easily, trouble concentrating, irritability, muscle tension and disturbed sleep. (Source: *Generalized anxiety disorder*, general clinical reference.)\n\n**How it differs from ordinary worry:** Anxiety is a normal response to threat. The reference describes a disorder as anxiety that is out of proportion to the situation, that lasts when the situation changes, and that gets in the way of daily functioning. So the main differences are:\n\n- **Duration:** Ordinary worry comes and goes. The disorder pattern is at least six months, most days.\n- **Intensity:** The worry is disproportionate to the circumstances.\n- **Impairment:** The worry is costly to work, relationships or daily life.\n- **Control:** The person finds it difficult to switch the worry off.\n\nThe retrieved material does not list the full formal diagnostic criteria, so I can't go further on those. A clinician would assess them.\n\n**Classification:** The ICD-10-CM chapter for mental, behavioral and neurodevelopmental disorders places generalized anxiety disorder among the anxiety and related disorders in the F40 to F48 range. The retrieved passages do not give the specific billing code, and the code lookup returned only chapter ranges, so I can't confirm the exact code here. Check the current ICD-10-CM tables or a coding reference for that.\n\nIf you are worried about your own experience, a doctor or mental health professional can assess whether these patterns apply to you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
